16
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:25,800
Okay, so lesson 18 is about integrating order flow with technical analysis.

17
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:29,800
Order flow can be used as a standalone way to analyze the market.

18
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:35,400
or you can use it to complement existing technical analysis that you currently use.

19
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:42,500
There's the term confluence, which is when you have two or more indicators giving the same market indication.

20
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:46,300
Many traders look for confluence in the primary and secondary indicators.

21
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:51,800
What that means is, you know, say you have two or more ways of analyzing the market.

22
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:54,700
When they're both going in the same direction, you have confluence.

23
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:59,700
So watching how the market reacts to your support and resistance levels.

24
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:03,200
Is price being rejected at those levels?

25
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:05,600
Are traders being aggressive getting through those levels?

26
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:12,700
So say you have a way of determining support and resistance levels in the market without using order flows.

27
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:18,900
Order flows will help you... Refine your entries and exits around those support and resistance levels.

28
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:32,200
By watching how the price is acting at those levels, you can see if price is being accepted or rejected, or if traders are being aggressive as you reach those levels, or if they're not being aggressive.

29
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:37,000
If they're being aggressive, you may have... You know, extended follow through through those levels.

30
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,400
So, you know, it's important to see not just that price traded your level, it's important to see how price reacted when you get to your levels.

31
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:53,500
So many veteran traders often view volume as a secondary indicator to help confirm other technical indicators on their chart.

32
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:59,500
Now, when I say veteran traders, I mean traders that have been trading, that have their way of trading.

33
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:04,600
for many years and sometimes they don't necessarily look at volume.

34
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:07,700
They have their own ways of analyzing the market.

35
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:16,200
If there's a big upside price breakout accompanying with heavy volume, the move is considered strong.

36
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:25,100
It's interesting when technical analysts don't look at volume, but then all of a sudden they see a big move and they see the volume is heavy.

37
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:27,500
All of a sudden, oh, you've got heavy volume.

38
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:38,600
It's interesting how they use it after the fact rather than using it during the normal course of trading.

39
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:45,600
So a big upside move or a move to a new high that is accompanied by light volume gives the impression that the move is weak or suspect.

40
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:50,200
So, you know, how can order flow help?

41
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:58,300
Order flow can help a trader gauge how aggressive traders are in the move. This is especially useful when a market starts to trend.

42
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:05,400
So, you know, when moves start, you have to take into account, you know, how moves start.

43
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:10,000
You know, everyone wants to get in on a move, but, you know, the earlier you get in on the move, the better.

44
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:15,400
So, what you want to notice is you start to move away from an area of...

45
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,100
You know, price acceptance, are traders being aggressive in the move?

46
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,300
If they are, then the move is likely to continue.

47
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:29,100
You know, are traders being aggressive in the opposite direction of a trend? If so, the market, the move may be about to end.

48
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:33,300
So, you know, what that means is, you know, say you're rallying and you hit a high, go sideways,

49
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:41,900
and you start to see sellers being aggressive on the sell side, then the move up is probably over.

50
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:48,200
It's one thing if it's going to continue to go down or just sort of hang around the high level.

51
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:52,500
I mean, there may be aggressive selling, but it may still be met with buying.

52
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,900
As people see the prices have moved higher and they're trying to also get in thinking, of a continuation.

53
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:07,600
It's when the sellers are going to be overwhelming the buyers that the move should end and start in a new direction.

54
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:15,000
So which indicators, forms of technical analysis work best when combined with order flow?

55
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:28,800
much most forms, support and resistance levels, pivot levels, Fibonacci levels, daily, weekly, monthly highs, lows, value areas and nodes, initial balance, trend lines.

56
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:31,600
It doesn't really matter what you use.

57
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:39,200
Sometimes I think you can just draw random lines on a chart in the markets. I'll say the market will react to them, but you'll see

58
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:46,000
There's going to be times when the markets seem to bounce off those levels, and it could be completely random.

59
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:48,700
It's sort of the right use.

60
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,500
It's not really a random walk theory, but it's one of those things.

61
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:58,500
You put 100 monkeys in a room and give them all typewriters, and eventually someone will write Hamlet.

62
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:02,300
You can have levels.

63
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:05,000
It doesn't really matter how you come up with them.

64
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:12,500
As long as you're convinced that your way of finding levels is the right way, watch how price reacts around them.

65
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:14,900
Watch the order flow and see what happens.

66
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:17,500
So when the market gets to your levels, is buying drying up?

67
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:18,400
Is selling drying up?

68
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:20,500
Is the market...

69
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:27,400
You know, trading imbalance or you've seen a lot of market imbalances in the form of, you know, aggressive selling, aggressive buying.

70
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:30,800
You know, what is the market doing, what it's supposed to do?

71
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:38,400
Those are all questions you should be asking when you're looking at, you know, your form of technical analysis when it's mixed with order flow.

72
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:45,700
Traditional lagging indicators such as moving averages, RSI, MACD do not really lend themselves well with order flows since they are based

73
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:51,800
Not too far in the past and not really levels as opposed to mathematical calculations based on price.

74
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:55,800
What I mean is you have a moving average, a 200-day moving average.

75
00:06:56,300 --> 00:06:57,900
Yeah, price can react around it.

76
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,600
Price does, obviously, but what are you using to calculate it?

77
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:03,000
It's a 200-day moving average.

78
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:05,400
You're going back 200 days in...

79
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:11,500
In your analysis, do you really want to go back 200 time periods?

80
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:22,000
If you're using a one-minute chart, you can go back 200 minutes, but you're going back quite a bit to sort of figure out your calculation based in the present.

81
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:25,800
That would be like looking at a bar chart and...

82
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:32,500
You know, sort of basing your analysis and your current price based on, you know, where you were three hours ago.

83
00:07:32,700 --> 00:07:34,200
Is it prudent? I don't know.

84
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:43,200
I'm not a big fan of moving averages and RSI and MACD, but, you know, if you are, the more power to you.

85
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:47,000
Try and mix order flow together with it.

86
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:49,500
I think order flow is better.

87
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:53,700
Watching how prices react around certain levels.

88
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:59,400
If you're getting up to an area where you think the market's overbought or oversold, yeah, watch the order flow.

89
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:05,300
See if buying or selling is drying up and see if traders are being aggressive at those levels.

90
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:11,200
Like I said, it doesn't really matter what other form of technical analysis you want to use with it.

91
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:20,000
With order flow, what you want to do is you want to apply what you know about order flow. flow in addition to your existing technical analysis.

92
00:08:21,500 --> 00:08:32,400
Now, one of the buzzwords in trading, besides order flow, order flow has become a buzzword in the last several years, but another one is VWAP, volume weighted average price.

93
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:41,000
I see people use the term VWAP, and that's the way institutions trade.

94
00:08:41,400 --> 00:08:54,000
However, the way that these people write about VWAP is they don't necessarily understand how it's used by institutional traders, and in turn, I think they have bastardized it into a glorified moving average.

95
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:01,800
VWAP is used by institutional traders as a measurement of trade execution, not as the level to buy or sell around.

96
00:09:04,356 --> 00:09:08,056
No one has gotten rich by beating VWAP except the brokers.

97
00:09:08,156 --> 00:09:13,656
Institutional traders are only concerned with the VWAP during the time the order is in the market getting filled.

98
00:09:14,656 --> 00:09:21,556
They measure their trade execution price against the VWAP from the beginning of the execution until the time it is filled.

99
00:09:21,656 --> 00:09:24,556
VWAP is not without its limitations as well.

100
00:09:25,356 --> 00:09:26,756
So what that means is...

101
00:09:28,656 --> 00:09:35,756
You know, a trader has an order, okay, sorry, a portfolio manager has an order, gives it to the execution trader to execute.

102
00:09:36,156 --> 00:09:44,356
He needs to buy 2,500 E-minis, and he'll say, you know, execute it over the next 30 minutes.

103
00:09:47,256 --> 00:09:51,256
The trader will execute it over the 30 minutes and he'll have his average price.

104
00:09:51,956 --> 00:10:03,156
Now what the portfolio manager will do to measure whether it's a good fill or a lousy fill is he'll take the VWAP from the time the order started executing to the time the order stopped executing.

105
00:10:03,656 --> 00:10:10,056
If his average price is below the VWAP, it's a good fill. It's above the VWAP. The trader did a lousy job of filling it.

106
00:10:10,456 --> 00:10:16,456
What he expects from the trader to do is he expects him to participate at the price levels that's trading.

107
00:10:16,556 --> 00:10:21,856
He doesn't want him to take a view on the market and, you know, sort of work it in when he feels like it.

108
00:10:21,956 --> 00:10:23,856
No, he wants to participate at all those levels.

109
00:10:23,856 --> 00:10:29,056
He wants a weighted average, you know, an average price based on the volume that's traded.

110
00:10:29,356 --> 00:10:34,856
So he wants to participate more when there's more trading. And, you know, less when there's less trading.

111
00:10:35,356 --> 00:10:45,356
So, you know, when I see people, you know, using VWAP in their trading, and they've got a VWAP running for the entire day, and they think, oh, there's two standard deviations from VWAP.

112
00:10:46,856 --> 00:10:53,356
You know, I sort of cringe at that because, you know, VWAP, like I said, VWAP has its limitations.

113
00:10:54,356 --> 00:10:58,456
You know, certain times of day inherently have more volume.

114
00:10:58,456 --> 00:11:02,256
You know, when the market opens, you know, it's going to be a lot of volume.

115
00:11:02,256 --> 00:11:04,656
When the cash session opens, it's going to be a lot of volume.

116
00:11:05,856 --> 00:11:13,456
You know, later in the day, the VWAP is going to get flatter. So if you have a move later in the day, it's going to have a smaller impact on VWAP.

117
00:11:13,756 --> 00:11:18,756
You know, for example, the minis trade, the e-minis, you know, will trade by 9 o'clock.

118
00:11:18,756 --> 00:11:21,356
You should have half a million contracts more or less traded.

119
00:11:22,156 --> 00:11:26,556
By 12 o'clock, all of a sudden, you have, you know, a million contracts traded.

120
00:11:26,956 --> 00:11:27,956
So...

121
00:11:28,756 --> 00:11:34,956
You know, take in the VWAP from the Globex open until 9 o'clock, you have half a million traded.

122
00:11:35,356 --> 00:11:39,656
But then over the next two hours, three hours, you got another half a million traded.

123
00:11:40,056 --> 00:11:46,956
Well, by the time you're going into the last two hours of the day, you've already got, you know, 70% of the volume traded.

124
00:11:47,056 --> 00:11:49,156
So your VWAP profile is going to get flatter.

125
00:11:50,556 --> 00:11:53,656
Also, depending on when you start your chart will have an effect on VWAP.

126
00:11:53,656 --> 00:11:59,856
I mean, do you want to take VWAP from, like I said, the beginning of the session at the Globex reopen in the evening?

127
00:12:00,356 --> 00:12:02,156
Or are you going to be taking it at the...

128
00:12:02,756 --> 00:12:08,256
you know, 830 when it opens, you know, either way, it's going to have an effect on how your VWAP is calculated.

129
00:12:08,756 --> 00:12:09,656
You know, that said,

130
00:12:12,056 --> 00:12:19,456
majority, I'll say the majority, but nearly all of institutional traders, when they look at VWAP, they're only looking at VWAP for that particular time the order is in the market.

131
00:12:19,956 --> 00:12:22,356
Like I said, they use it as a measure of execution.

132
00:12:22,856 --> 00:12:25,156
They don't use it as a way to trade around.

133
00:12:25,356 --> 00:12:32,656
Some people probably do now that it's so much, you know, word around it, you know, so much talk about it.

134
00:12:32,656 --> 00:12:44,756
But, you know, the algos that trade around VWAP, you know, they're a completely different style of trading than you and I, you know, as retail traders.

135
00:12:46,756 --> 00:12:53,556
So, I mean, you know, probably earlier in the day, BWAP is going to fluctuate more than it is going to be later in the day.

136
00:12:53,956 --> 00:13:03,756
You know, if you've traded already a million and a half contracts, it's not going to have much effect on the next, you know, 200,000 contracts as you're going into the close.

137
00:13:03,756 --> 00:13:06,956
So, you know, it just pains me to see.

138
00:13:07,456 --> 00:13:16,656
You know, people that have never worked on the institutional side start talking about VWAP and they don't necessarily understand how it's used on the institutional side.

139
00:13:16,656 --> 00:13:22,756
You know, they see it as an indicator and they think, oh yeah, I could use that, but they're using it

140
00:13:24,056 --> 00:13:26,856
in a way that's not being used by the institutional traders.

141
00:13:26,856 --> 00:13:32,256
And like I said, they just sort of turned it into a form.

142
00:13:32,356 --> 00:13:38,656
of a moving average and then they start talking about when you move one or two standard deviations from it, you know, that price should go back to it.

143
00:13:38,656 --> 00:13:42,656
Well, you know, it doesn't necessarily always work that way.

144
00:13:44,256 --> 00:13:50,156
So, you know, anyway, enough about, you know, my problem with the way people use VWeb.

145
00:13:50,256 --> 00:13:51,356
Let's take a look at...

146
00:13:53,256 --> 00:13:56,956
Technical analysis and order flows.

147
00:13:56,956 --> 00:13:59,356
So this is a Fibonacci chart.

148
00:13:59,756 --> 00:14:03,256
Well, this is the order flows chart with Fibonacci retracements laid into it.

149
00:14:03,256 --> 00:14:05,756
I like to use 50% as a pullback.

150
00:14:07,356 --> 00:14:09,756
You know, others, you know, 38% or 61%.

151
00:14:10,256 --> 00:14:10,856
But what do you have?

152
00:14:10,856 --> 00:14:15,756
You know, as you get up to the 50% area, you're starting to see some small prints.

153
00:14:17,256 --> 00:14:25,356
So, you know, it's a sign that buying has dried up on this pullback in there and, you know, look for the move to come back down.

154
00:14:25,756 --> 00:14:30,356
Even at the 38%, you're seeing a selling imbalance up here, which is also, you know,

155
00:14:32,756 --> 00:14:35,256
a sign that the market should sell off.

156
00:14:36,556 --> 00:14:42,956
Again, this is E-mini chart, this is a 5 range and It's also Fibonacci retracements.

157
00:14:43,056 --> 00:14:50,956
You know, you have your high up here, you have your low down here. And, you know, drawing the, you know, this is a standard one on Ninja Trader.

158
00:14:50,956 --> 00:14:55,656
You could just... Couple clicks and most software has it. You don't need anything fancy to use it.

159
00:14:56,456 --> 00:15:01,756
You know, it came right back up to 50%, started coming down and you can see here at the 38%.

160
00:15:02,456 --> 00:15:06,656
you have a single print in a ratio and the market sold off some more.

161
00:15:09,256 --> 00:15:10,956
I'm not a big fan of 61%.

162
00:15:10,956 --> 00:15:15,856
Usually I prefer 50%, but sometimes it's quite close.

163
00:15:15,856 --> 00:15:18,756
It's not much of a difference between 50 to 61.

164
00:15:18,956 --> 00:15:21,056
It might be a point or something or a few ticks.

165
00:15:21,556 --> 00:15:29,356
Depending on where your highs and lows are that you're using for it, I try to get something where...

166
00:15:30,156 --> 00:15:42,556
It's a decent move, probably 6, 7, 8 and above points before I start getting interested in drawing any sort of retracements intraday.

167
00:15:46,724 --> 00:15:52,224
This one is basically around a five-point move, and it's a five-range chart.

168
00:15:52,224 --> 00:15:58,124
So from your high to your low, you come back up to just about right at the 50% level.

169
00:15:58,424 --> 00:16:03,724
Again, you get a single print, and the market sells off basically another five points.

170
00:16:07,424 --> 00:16:10,824
This is a trend line for those that like to draw trend lines.

171
00:16:11,224 --> 00:16:12,824
The trend lines are a bit subjective.

172
00:16:14,824 --> 00:16:20,824
They're not something that is necessarily easy for people to draw.

173
00:16:22,024 --> 00:16:25,824
You could give a chart to 10 people, you'd probably get 9 different trend lines drawn.

174
00:16:25,924 --> 00:16:27,424
I'll say 9, probably about 7.

175
00:16:29,524 --> 00:16:30,024
It's hard.

176
00:16:30,324 --> 00:16:37,424
I have my way of drawing trend lines. I don't necessarily like to just cover it down. I like to go from a high to a high.

177
00:16:38,424 --> 00:16:40,624
I don't necessarily need to have so many points in a trend line.

178
00:16:40,824 --> 00:16:46,724
If it's a wide enough area, so from this high to this high, you come off, that's going to be my point in the trend line.

179
00:16:47,724 --> 00:16:50,524
Some people try to draw it a little bit steeper coming down here.

180
00:16:52,724 --> 00:16:57,924
The steeper you go, sometimes the more violations of the trend line you're going to have.

181
00:16:59,224 --> 00:17:05,424
I don't want to get into a dissertation on drawing trend lines, but watch what happens as you come off a trend line.

182
00:17:07,124 --> 00:17:11,224
And you start to go through it, is there, you know, towards the bottom,

183
00:17:13,024 --> 00:17:21,724
you start coming back up to the trend line, is there any sort of ratios in there, anything to get you interested in buying the breakout?

184
00:17:22,624 --> 00:17:24,824
So I see here, I see a ratio.

185
00:17:25,324 --> 00:17:27,124
Get to the other side, it's met with some ratios.

186
00:17:27,124 --> 00:17:31,824
Then it starts to come down again, and you've got some ratios stopping it.

187
00:17:31,824 --> 00:17:35,624
So for me, this is a valid trend line break.

188
00:17:42,924 --> 00:17:47,524
Okay, well, this is a chart to let order order, but this is a Fibonacci again.

189
00:17:48,224 --> 00:17:55,824
And, you know, from this high down to this low, we've, you know, come back up just past the 50% line.

190
00:17:56,524 --> 00:18:01,124
First time you had a, you have a ratio, but then you sort of pop up and you get to the 61% line.

191
00:18:02,024 --> 00:18:15,524
Then you come back down and at the 50% line you get the small single print again, which is telling you that it looks like now the buying has dried up and the market is starting to react to go lower.

192
00:18:18,852 --> 00:18:21,252
Okay, this next chart is Bollinger Bands.

193
00:18:24,152 --> 00:18:34,252
And, you know, when you're trading Bollinger Bands, you want to see when you come up to the upper band, what's going to happen, or the lower band.

194
00:18:34,952 --> 00:18:39,452
And you come up here and you're seeing a ratio. You're seeing stopping volume.

195
00:18:39,752 --> 00:18:44,352
And we trade right back into the middle band and onto the lower band.

196
00:18:47,252 --> 00:18:51,952
Again, you trade from the lower band up to the higher band and this time you have a ratio.

197
00:18:53,352 --> 00:19:05,352
The thing with bands, you're trading in sort of, you say you have these bands, you want to see how when you touch the bands, if there's any sign, it's going to be lots of times, there's no

198
00:19:06,352 --> 00:19:10,352
No sign, like here you don't have any signal with any ratio that you like.

199
00:19:10,752 --> 00:19:15,052
You know, you rallied up to the next band, but you do have a ratio and the market reacted.

200
00:19:15,552 --> 00:19:23,052
Because there's going to be times when you're trading inside bands where you're touching, you know, this is the previous chart, you know, you hit the upper band, but nothing.

201
00:19:23,452 --> 00:19:24,952
You sell, you hit the upper band, nothing.

202
00:19:24,952 --> 00:19:28,752
You sell, you hit the upper band. Now you have a ratio and the market comes off.

203
00:19:35,368 --> 00:19:37,668
This one is, again, it's a Bollinger Band.

204
00:19:39,068 --> 00:19:41,068
I'm not the type that likes to trade around the center line.

205
00:19:43,968 --> 00:19:47,568
Because you could be on the other side, both sides of a center line in a bar, and it's hard.

206
00:19:49,568 --> 00:19:55,368
But what's happened is you've come down into this lower band here.

207
00:19:58,168 --> 00:20:03,668
You know, between the center band and the lower band, you have a selling imbalance. And then, you know, you don't have anything.

208
00:20:03,668 --> 00:20:07,668
You touched the lower band here, but you don't have any clue to really get long.

209
00:20:07,668 --> 00:20:11,868
You do have a divergence, but, I mean, you can't just get long on every divergence.

210
00:20:12,668 --> 00:20:17,168
And you rally back up into the selling imbalance, and you start coming back off.

211
00:20:17,168 --> 00:20:23,268
You hit the lower band again with the single print in the bar with the single print, and the market goes lower.

212
00:20:24,368 --> 00:20:28,368
You know, it's... There's all sorts of examples.

213
00:20:28,568 --> 00:20:32,068
I could give you examples all day long, but I'm just giving you a few.

214
00:20:32,768 --> 00:20:34,868
Here you have, again, it's a Bollinger Band.

215
00:20:34,868 --> 00:20:35,968
You hit the lower band.

216
00:20:36,568 --> 00:20:48,768
You have a ratio, and you have a selling and balance at the low, and you trade right up to the middle band and up to the top band, where it sort of stops up there.

217
00:20:48,868 --> 00:20:50,768
You have another ratio.

218
00:20:51,868 --> 00:20:58,568
What you want to see is if you're picking your levels, say you have a way of trading in technical analysis,

219
00:20:59,268 --> 00:21:07,468
giving you some sort of levels, you know, try and look at order flow with it and how price is reacting during, you know, around your levels.

220
00:21:08,568 --> 00:21:11,368
You know, I'm not a person that

221
00:21:13,768 --> 00:21:15,568
is looking to get in on every single move.

222
00:21:15,568 --> 00:21:20,368
I'm just looking for the times where Orderful confirms what I think should happen.

223
00:21:21,268 --> 00:21:29,268
I said if you're trading, say, Bollinger Bands, you're not always selling whenever you hit the high band and buying whenever you hit the low band.

224
00:21:29,268 --> 00:21:35,568
You want to buy... At the low band, when you have a reason to buy, you want to sell at the high band when you have a reason to sell.

225
00:21:35,968 --> 00:21:42,868
You don't want to just keep selling because you go from one standard deviation to two standard deviations to three standard deviations.

226
00:21:43,768 --> 00:21:48,068
And then you're broke after you hit the fourth standard deviation because you kept selling it.

227
00:21:48,068 --> 00:21:50,968
It was looking for the reversion back to the mean, and it never happened.

228
00:21:52,468 --> 00:21:56,668
Okay, this is a 200-day moving average, this green line.

229
00:21:57,368 --> 00:22:02,768
And I said, I'm not a big fan of moving averages because, you know, sometimes the market will go and just sort of trade around it.

230
00:22:03,068 --> 00:22:07,468
But then, you know, what happens as you're trading around it, but then you see the buyers come in.

231
00:22:07,568 --> 00:22:10,868
You see, you know, just on the other side of the...

232
00:22:11,568 --> 00:22:14,368
200-day moving average, which is a long-term moving average.

233
00:22:14,968 --> 00:22:16,368
So is it important?

234
00:22:16,868 --> 00:22:23,568
Yeah, probably more important than, say, a 40-day moving average, just because it's taking a lot more things into account.

235
00:22:23,668 --> 00:22:27,068
But still, you want to see how price reacts around it.

236
00:22:28,068 --> 00:22:29,168
There's no price re-

237
00:22:29,168 --> 00:22:39,668
ejection here and what you have is you trade up from below it up through it and then all of a sudden you see more aggressive buying come in just on the other side of it to continue it in that direction

238
00:22:42,368 --> 00:22:45,268
Okay, so this is, again, the 200-day moving average.

239
00:22:45,268 --> 00:22:46,268
And what are you seeing?

240
00:22:46,368 --> 00:22:48,068
You're seeing you're starting to move towards it.

241
00:22:48,168 --> 00:22:55,068
And you're really seeing all the single prints line up and you break through it on a single print.

242
00:22:55,068 --> 00:22:58,968
And just all around it, you have selling imbalances.

243
00:22:58,968 --> 00:23:06,168
So it's telling me the market is more than likely to break through that level.

244
00:23:10,608 --> 00:23:19,808
So this is the continuation of this chart is A, this chart I think is B, because this is just the next few minutes.

245
00:23:19,808 --> 00:23:26,408
And what happens? It does come right down through it, rallies back up towards it, and continues down lower.

246
00:23:26,908 --> 00:23:34,408
So it wasn't like the strongest move, but you're seeing here early signs that as you're going to be making the run towards that,

247
00:23:35,408 --> 00:23:42,008
average that there's some aggressive selling coming in and there's price rejection up here in the form of these single prints.

248
00:23:42,608 --> 00:23:48,008
So, you know, I think it's, you know, it's a high probability trade that you're going to get past this level.

249
00:23:48,208 --> 00:23:55,108
And, you know, instead of this level acting as support, once you get past it, it'll be a resistance level, which is exactly what happened.

250
00:23:55,508 --> 00:24:07,908
So, you know, even if you're not yet convinced that this is, you know, a true breakdown, you throw an offer up around the average and look for a pullback in there and a continuation lower.

251
00:24:08,808 --> 00:24:16,108
So, I mean, he could have got at least a few points out of it in a very quick manner.

252
00:24:20,460 --> 00:24:23,360
So this one ends at 839. What is that, the 20th?

253
00:24:24,560 --> 00:24:26,060
So this is about 10 minutes later.

254
00:24:26,360 --> 00:24:30,160
So we come back up, go through it, and we're just sort of hanging around it.

255
00:24:30,460 --> 00:24:36,060
And you're not seeing anything necessarily as you're hanging around it.

256
00:24:36,060 --> 00:24:42,260
It's when you get to this area, you're seeing now you're seeing the price rejection again in the form of the small print.

257
00:24:42,260 --> 00:24:45,560
You don't necessarily have it here. Here or here.

258
00:24:46,360 --> 00:24:50,760
You know, I said you're seeing some buying imbalances, but it's meant with selling imbalances.

259
00:24:51,560 --> 00:25:02,060
You know, you're thinking here maybe you got this big buying imbalance, a thousand just on the other side of the average, but you know, you can only get up two or three points and now you're seeing a single print

260
00:25:02,560 --> 00:25:11,360
with the ratio, you know, basically indicating that there's really not much interest up here above this, you know, this 45 and a half, 46 and above level.

261
00:25:15,320 --> 00:25:17,920
You know, then what happens? Again, the market sells off.

262
00:25:19,920 --> 00:25:28,020
So, you know, that's one of the problems with trading with moving averages is they often quite sort of will, I mean, it acts as a magnet.

263
00:25:28,120 --> 00:25:32,020
And then once it's around there, it just really can go sideways on you sometimes.

264
00:25:32,120 --> 00:25:33,720
And, you know, you're going to get chopped up.

265
00:25:34,020 --> 00:25:37,120
I said I'm not a big fan of moving averages, but...

266
00:25:37,820 --> 00:25:42,920
You can do some analysis of order flows around them.

267
00:25:43,420 --> 00:25:44,620
So here's a trend line again.

268
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:52,400
It's drawn from the low here.

269
00:25:52,700 --> 00:25:54,600
So you got your couple points, one, two.

270
00:25:56,500 --> 00:25:59,600
Up here, you're well above the trend line. You have the high.

271
00:25:59,600 --> 00:26:02,700
Okay, you're looking for a break. You get the little breakthrough here.

272
00:26:03,100 --> 00:26:07,400
Maybe you're not that... You don't want to just sell the trend line breakout.

273
00:26:07,400 --> 00:26:11,400
You want to have a reason. To be short once you break the trend line.

274
00:26:11,500 --> 00:26:12,500
And here's your reason.

275
00:26:12,500 --> 00:26:13,700
You've got the selling imbalance.

276
00:26:17,200 --> 00:26:20,500
You're seeing these ratios here to hide behind.

277
00:26:20,600 --> 00:26:26,400
You know, if you're getting short in this area, you know, you get a nice little move lower.

278
00:26:30,368 --> 00:26:37,268
Okay, this one is a little bit more, I'll say esoteric, but it's a GAN fan.

279
00:26:38,568 --> 00:26:41,668
You could Google GAN fans and try to understand them.

280
00:26:42,468 --> 00:26:48,468
But for me, the way I view it is sort of a fancy trend lines.

281
00:26:49,168 --> 00:26:50,768
It's like an array of trend lines.

282
00:26:51,268 --> 00:26:55,268
But what have I always found important with...

283
00:26:55,568 --> 00:27:00,468
The GAN fans, is the 45 degree line, which is this one in the middle, the one by one line.

284
00:27:01,568 --> 00:27:02,968
To me, that's the most important.

285
00:27:03,868 --> 00:27:09,168
A lot of people will look at this 2x1 or this 4x1.

286
00:27:10,568 --> 00:27:11,868
Price will hold the 4x1.

287
00:27:11,968 --> 00:27:13,668
The one that fails, it will hold the 2x1.

288
00:27:13,668 --> 00:27:15,268
The one that fails, it will hold the 1x1.

289
00:27:15,468 --> 00:27:19,768
But for me, the most important one is I've always found it, and I did.

290
00:27:20,168 --> 00:27:24,468
Do a study into Gantt stuff earlier in my career back in the 90s.

291
00:27:26,768 --> 00:27:32,068
For me, I've sort of carried the one-by-one line over to my normal trading.

292
00:27:33,568 --> 00:27:34,768
It's a level I'm always looking at.

293
00:27:34,868 --> 00:27:36,868
Basically, it's the 45-degree angle line.

294
00:27:37,768 --> 00:27:42,068
And I'm always interested if we're going to break that for the breakdown to occur.

295
00:27:42,668 --> 00:27:49,568
And here I see at the 45 degree line the single print bar with the ratio that I like.

296
00:27:49,568 --> 00:27:56,668
So to me it's indicating that if we could break this level, this 45 degree line, we should stay below it and go lower.

297
00:28:00,368 --> 00:28:06,968
But for me now, the 45 degree line, I don't necessarily draw the angles on the chart. It's more of a visual thing.

298
00:28:08,868 --> 00:28:17,568
Now, this is the Andrews Pitchfork, and it's an interesting tool.

299
00:28:18,368 --> 00:28:20,568
It's an old-timers tool.

300
00:28:20,668 --> 00:28:23,768
It's a tool used by people that...

301
00:28:26,168 --> 00:28:28,168
It's sort of been around the markets for a long time.

302
00:28:28,668 --> 00:28:30,468
You sort of see it rehashed nowadays.

303
00:28:30,968 --> 00:28:38,168
But, you know, a lot of people that trade eggs followed it back, you know, 20, 30 years ago.

304
00:28:38,268 --> 00:28:39,368
But it's still followed today.

305
00:28:39,468 --> 00:28:46,968
And it is an interesting little technical analysis piece.

306
00:28:48,468 --> 00:28:54,168
Basically, you have a channel here and you're looking for the breakout again of the pitchfork. It's shaped like a pitchfork.

307
00:28:54,668 --> 00:29:02,668
So here, you're inside this lower channel. So you're going to be looking for a breakout.

308
00:29:02,968 --> 00:29:08,468
And what's the first sign is maybe we could be close to a breakout. You have this selling imbalance.

309
00:29:08,468 --> 00:29:11,568
So if you could break out through there, where does it go?

310
00:29:11,768 --> 00:29:17,668
It sells off nicely down to basically a four or five point sell off.

311
00:29:19,668 --> 00:29:25,068
It's an indicator that comes with most trading software.

312
00:29:25,468 --> 00:29:30,268
I said I use NinjaCator. Sorry, Ninja Trader. And it's on there.

313
00:29:31,268 --> 00:29:34,468
Just right-click on your chart, and you can pull it up.

314
00:29:35,668 --> 00:29:39,168
Again, this is more on a longer-term chart.

315
00:29:39,468 --> 00:29:48,868
And what you want to see is when you're touching these areas, the channels, how price is reacting.

316
00:29:50,168 --> 00:29:55,568
Here you get on the other side of it, but you got a ratio, which is bullish.

317
00:29:55,568 --> 00:30:01,668
You trade right back into it, so maybe you didn't catch that move, because it's a little bit on the outside.

318
00:30:01,968 --> 00:30:08,068
I don't necessarily know if I would have been getting short there. If I was, when I see this ratio, I'd be looking to get long again.

319
00:30:09,568 --> 00:30:12,368
You know, we're trading up to the middle. I'm not a fan of trading in the middle.

320
00:30:12,668 --> 00:30:17,168
Then we start touching it again here. This is around the 46 level.

321
00:30:18,568 --> 00:30:22,368
And you see in ratios, it just trades right back up into it again.

322
00:30:23,268 --> 00:30:28,268
So when you're touching the bottom of a channel, watch what price is doing.

323
00:30:32,056 --> 00:30:35,656
Okay, now this is on a bond. This is, you know, as the market went down.

324
00:30:35,656 --> 00:30:42,556
You got your pitchfork here. You're trading at the upper end of the channel. And you start to break out.

325
00:30:42,656 --> 00:30:50,456
And what happens when you break out? You broke out here, but you traded back inside. But now it's giving you a sign that it wants to break out again.

326
00:30:50,556 --> 00:30:51,456
And you have a ratio.

327
00:30:53,056 --> 00:30:58,356
You got a small print forming there, and the market did break out of it, but it went mostly sideways.

328
00:30:59,156 --> 00:31:01,356
That's a light win.

329
00:31:03,756 --> 00:31:05,256
A couple ticks is a couple ticks.

330
00:31:07,256 --> 00:31:12,756
Not every winner is going to be something you can go on vacation with, but you add enough of them together.

331
00:31:14,056 --> 00:31:14,756
Then that's great.

332
00:31:15,156 --> 00:31:20,456
So these are just a few of the great ways you can integrate order flow with traditional technical analysis.

333
00:31:20,856 --> 00:31:26,956
Personally, I'm not one to use many indicators on my charts due to the cluttered feeling it gives me. And I prefer to keep it simple.

334
00:31:27,356 --> 00:31:35,356
However, I know there are traders killing it out there because they use order flow in their own technical analysis and are kept out of a lot of bad trades.

335
00:31:35,756 --> 00:31:39,256
Like I said, say you have your own support and resistance lines, but

336
00:31:39,956 --> 00:31:42,956
You notice half the time they work, half the time they don't work.

337
00:31:42,956 --> 00:31:48,456
What you want to do is you want to refine it and get more of the times that they do work and less of the times they don't work.

338
00:31:49,856 --> 00:31:52,356
I do think order flows can help you in that regards.

339
00:31:52,856 --> 00:31:55,756
I have customers that came to me.

340
00:31:57,356 --> 00:31:58,656
Wanted to learn order flow.

341
00:31:59,056 --> 00:32:09,756
Specifically because they know how price reacts around their levels, but sometimes it works, sometimes it doesn't work, and they want to refine their entry levels.

342
00:32:11,256 --> 00:32:17,256
Or learn to stay in a trade maybe a little bit longer when a trade is right based on their analysis.

343
00:32:17,256 --> 00:32:24,356
So they'll use it in two ways. One, they'll use it to refine their entries, and two, they'll use it to try and get a little bit more out of the trade.

344
00:32:24,656 --> 00:32:26,556
Like I said, if you can add an extra tick,

345
00:32:27,756 --> 00:32:34,356
you know, to every trade at the end of a day that, you know, that could add up at the end of a week, at the end of a month, at the end of the year.

346
00:32:34,356 --> 00:32:39,056
It could be a lot of money, you know, and you shouldn't sneeze at one tick.

347
00:32:40,156 --> 00:32:44,156
So, I mean, I said just making one extra tick, imagine what sort of difference that could make in your trading.

348
00:32:44,756 --> 00:32:51,556
So that's the end of lesson number 18, integrating order flows with traditional technical analysis.

349
00:32:52,256 --> 00:32:59,956
In the next lesson, I'll let you apply all that you've learned via a series of charts in different markets on different days. So you can see the thinking process as the day unfolds.

350
00:33:00,056 --> 00:33:04,056
This next lesson, lesson 19, is going to be a long one.

351
00:33:05,656 --> 00:33:13,256
Just because I'm going to show you charts on an intraday basis and sort of let you digest it, then I'll discuss it.

352
00:33:13,256 --> 00:33:16,356
So thank you. That's the end of Lesson 18.

